---
slug: "security"
title: "Permissions and secrets"
description: "Make the ownership boundary visible, keep credentials out of browser bundles, and preserve useful failures."
section: "Organization & security"
order: 45
---

## Begin at the boundary

A workspace, tenant or project identifies the resources an action is allowed to affect. A display name alone does not establish permission. Send the exact resource identity required by the contract.

## Put credentials in the right place

Server credentials stay on the server. Browser integrations should use limited credentials intended for that surface. Do not include secrets in query strings, copied examples or source control.

## Preserve refusal

A failed or refused operation is part of the system’s behavior. Show the reason and the next useful action instead of changing an error into a generic success state.

## Local project behavior

Learning progress stays in the current browser. The sample sandbox API stores only synthetic resources in memory. The optional model key is read only by the server-side assistant route. No MIOSA account is connected.
